  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=5{x}^{3}+35{x}^{2}+2x+14$ has a root in the interval $(-15,-6),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$",
  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $14$ and the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of\u00a0}14}{\\text{factor of\u00a0}5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 7,\\pm 14,\\pm \\frac{1}{5},\\pm \\frac{2}{5},\\pm \\frac{7}{5},\\pm \\frac{14}{5}.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-15,-6),$ are $-14$ and $-7.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+7)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-7$ | $5$ | $35$ | $2$ | $14$ |\n|  | $\u2193$ | $-35$ | $0$ | $-14$ |\n|  | $5$ | $0$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+7)$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+7)(5{x}^{2}+2).\n$$\n\nSince $5{x}^{2}+2\\ne 0$ for any real number $x,$ the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=-7.$",
